      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Rain on a generator might ruin the generator or give you a shock. A generator inside to keep it out of the rain will kill everyone in the house. Between those two, the choice is not close — so the real job is keeping it dry outdoors.
        </p>

        <h2 id="manufacturer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What the Manufacturers Say, and Why
        </h2>

        <p>
          Open the manual for any portable generator — a Honda EU2200i, a Champion dual-fuel, a Generac GP series, a Westinghouse, a DuroMax, a Predator from Harbor Freight — and you will find the same warning in slightly different words: do not operate in rain, snow or wet conditions, and do not expose the unit to moisture. Some go further and say do not operate with wet hands. This is not lawyer boilerplate. There are three real reasons behind it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Electrocution risk at the outlets</h3>
        <p>
          A generator control panel is a cluster of 120 and 240 volt receptacles, a main breaker, sometimes a GFCI, and on larger units a 30 or 50 amp twist-lock outlet. Water on that panel bridges contacts and finds paths to the frame. Water inside a plugged-in cord end does the same. A person touching a wet generator or a wet plug while standing on wet ground is the textbook setup for a fatal shock, and the generator's frame is only as grounded as you made it. Every year there are electrocutions during outages, and wet conditions are a factor in most of them.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Water in the alternator and controls</h3>
        <p>
          On an open-frame generator, the alternator end is often protected by little more than a slotted plastic cover. Water entering it shorts windings, corrodes the brushes and slip rings, and on an inverter unit can destroy the electronics module, which is the single most expensive part of the machine. The engine side is more tolerant — a lawn mower runs in damp grass — but water down the air intake or into the fuel tank causes rough running, stalling and eventual corrosion in the carburettor.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Liability</h3>
        <p>
          The manufacturers know perfectly well that people run these things in storms. They cannot say "here is how" without accepting liability for whatever goes wrong, so the manual says "do not". The gap between the manual and reality is the whole reason this guide exists.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The reality: outages happen in storms</h3>
        <p>
          Roughly speaking, the weather causes the outage. Ice storms bring down lines, hurricanes and derechos flatten poles, summer thunderstorms take out transformers. If your generator can only run in dry weather, it is not a backup power system; it is a camping accessory. So the honest position — and the one we take — is that you can and will run a portable generator in the rain, and the job is to do it in a way that keeps water off the electrical parts while keeping the exhaust and the heat completely unconfined. Those two requirements pull in opposite directions, and nearly every dangerous setup we have seen comes from prioritising the first at the expense of the second.
        </p>

        <h2 id="safe-solutions" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. The Safe Solutions
        </h2>

        <p>
          What every acceptable arrangement has in common: a roof over the generator, nothing around it. Air needs to reach the intake and the cooling fan from all sides, and exhaust needs to leave freely and dilute in open air. The roof keeps the rain off. The open sides keep you alive.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Purpose-built running canopies</h3>
        <p>
          The best solution for most people is a canopy designed specifically to be used on a running generator. GenTent is the one that established the category: a weatherproof fabric canopy on a frame that clamps to the generator's own tubular frame, sits a few inches above the unit, and covers the top and drapes partway down the sides while leaving the bottom fully open. It comes in sizes for open-frame generators from about 3,000 to 10,000 watts and in a version for inverter units. IGAN and a few others make similar clamp-on tents at lower prices, and Champion, Westinghouse and Generac sell running covers matched to their own models. Prices run from around 100 to 250 dollars.
        </p>

        <p>
          These work because they were engineered around the two constraints. The canopy sheds rain off the control panel and the alternator. The gap between the canopy and the machine, and the open lower sides, let the engine pull cooling air and let exhaust and heat escape. Some models include a flap that lets you reach the panel and plug in cords under cover. They are rated for real weather — GenTent quotes wind ratings in the 70 mph range and tests in snow load — and they travel with the generator rather than needing a spot in the yard. If you own a portable generator for outage duty and have not bought one of these, it is the single most useful accessory you can add.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A permanent open-sided shelter</h3>
        <p>
          If you always run the generator in the same spot, a small roof on posts — think of a very small carport or a lean-to with no walls — is an excellent long-term solution. Build it with clearances in mind: the roof at least 4 to 5 feet above the top of the generator so heat and exhaust do not pool under it, all four sides completely open, at least 5 feet of clear space between the generator and any post, wall or stored material, and non-combustible roofing or a fire-rated underside. Locate it 20 feet or more from the house and from any neighbour's house, with the exhaust side facing away from every window, door and vent. A gravel or paver pad under it keeps the unit out of mud and standing water. This is also a good place to run a buried feeder to an inlet box if you use a transfer switch or interlock — see our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types</a> for how that connection should be done.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pop-up canopies without side walls</h3>
        <p>
          A standard 10 by 10 foot pop-up canopy, the sort sold for tailgating, works in a pinch if you use it correctly. That means: no side walls at all, not even one; the generator centred so exhaust and heat have several feet before they reach a leg or the canopy edge; the canopy staked and weighted, because storm wind will otherwise turn it into a kite that lands on the generator; and an eye on the fabric above the exhaust, which can get hot enough to damage cheap polyester if the canopy is low. It is a stopgap, not a system, but it is far better than a tarp or nothing.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Get it off the ground</h3>
        <p>
          Whichever cover you use, do not let the generator sit in a puddle. Standing water around the base wets the frame, wicks into cords lying on the ground, and if it rises far enough reaches the outlets on low-mounted panels. Set the unit on a couple of concrete pavers, a section of composite decking, a pallet, or simply the highest well-drained spot in the yard. Keep the exhaust pointed downhill and away from the house.
        </p>

        <h2 id="never" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Where a Generator Must Never Run
        </h2>

        <p>
          Carbon monoxide is the issue here, and it is worth being blunt about the numbers. A typical 5,000 watt portable generator produces as much CO as several hundred idling cars. CO is colourless, odourless, and lighter than most people assume, so it drifts and spreads rather than pooling neatly. The CPSC counts roughly 85 CO deaths a year in the US from portable generators, and the CDC's investigations of storm-related deaths find the same pattern every time: the generator was in a garage, a basement, an enclosed porch, or right outside an open window.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Never in a garage, even with the door open</h3>
        <p>
          This is the most common fatal mistake and it feels reasonable from the inside. The garage is dry, the door is open, the exhaust is pointed out. It does not work. An open garage door does not create anything like enough airflow to clear the output of a running engine, CO accumulates along the ceiling and the back of the garage, and it moves into the house through the connecting door, around the door frame, and through any duct or gap in the shared wall. People have died in bedrooms on the far side of the house from a generator in an open garage. There is no arrangement of fans or doors that makes this safe.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Never in a shed, a box or any enclosure</h3>
        <p>
          The plastic garden shed with the doors open, the plywood "generator box" with vent holes, the dog house, the crawl space, the space under a deck with lattice on the sides — all of these concentrate CO and heat around the machine. The heat alone will cook an air-cooled engine and can melt a plastic shed; the CO turns any enclosure into a hazard for the people around it. A generator shelter has a roof and no walls. If it has walls, it is not a shelter, it is a trap.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Never near windows, doors or vents</h3>
        <p>
          The CDC and CPSC both say at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust directed away. Earlier guidance of 10 or 15 feet has been revised upward because CO measured inside homes at those distances was still dangerous. Keep the same distance from the neighbour's house. Check for dryer vents, soffit vents, crawl space vents and window air conditioners on the side you are using, because those all draw outside air in. Wind matters too: in a storm, exhaust that would normally drift away can be blown straight back against the house, which is why 20 feet is a minimum and CO alarms indoors are non-negotiable.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Never on a porch or balcony</h3>
        <p>
          A covered porch feels like the perfect compromise: dry, outdoors, close to the door. It is not outdoors in any way that matters. The porch roof and the house wall trap exhaust, the door is right there, and the windows above it are the bedroom windows. Same for a balcony, a breezeway or the covered area between a house and detached garage.
        </p>

        <h2 id="types" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Open-Frame, Inverter and Standby
        </h2>

        <p>
          How much a generator minds getting wet depends on what type it is, and it is worth understanding the differences before the storm rather than during it. Our <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby comparison</a> covers the broader trade-offs; here is the weather-specific version.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Portable open-frame generators</h3>
        <p>
          The classic contractor-style unit: a steel tube frame, an exposed engine, an alternator with a plastic end cover, and a control panel bolted to the frame. Everything is reachable by rain from above and by splash from the side. The panel is usually the lowest and most exposed electrical component. These are the units that most need a running canopy, and they are also the units the canopies are designed to clamp onto. Open-frame generators are the most common choice for whole-house outage duty because of their wattage per dollar, so if you have one of these, budget for the cover as part of the purchase.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Inverter generators</h3>
        <p>
          A Honda EU2200i, Yamaha EF2200iS, Champion 2500 or similar has its engine and alternator inside a moulded plastic housing. That housing sheds a fair amount of rain by itself, and the outlets are usually on a vertical panel that is somewhat sheltered. In our experience they tolerate a light shower better than an open-frame machine. They are still not rain-rated. The housing has intake and exhaust openings that water can enter, the panel is exposed to wind-driven rain, and the inverter module inside is the most water-sensitive component on any generator. Treat an inverter generator as slightly more forgiving, not as weatherproof, and cover it the same way. GenTent and others make canopies specifically for the enclosed-housing shape. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generator guide</a> covers the models.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Standby generators</h3>
        <p>
          A permanently installed Generac Guardian, Kohler, Briggs and Stratton or Cummins standby unit lives outside on a pad inside an aluminium or steel enclosure that is designed to sit in rain, snow, salt air and heat for fifteen or twenty years. Rain is simply not a concern. The unit starts itself within seconds of an outage, transfers the house over automatically, and the electrical connections are all inside the enclosure and the transfer switch. The only weather-related maintenance is keeping the intake and exhaust louvres clear of snow drifts, leaves and shrubs, and maintaining the clearances the installer set — typically 18 inches from the house and 5 feet from openings, per the manufacturer's listing. If the "generator in the rain" problem is one you face every year, a standby unit is the permanent answer, and our <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">whole-house generator guide</a> and <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac cost breakdown</a> cover what that involves.
        </p>

        <h2 id="electrical" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. GFCI Trips, Cords and Connections
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the GFCI trips in humid or wet weather</h3>
        <p>
          Most portable generators sold in the US since the early 2010s have GFCI protection on their 120 volt duplex outlets, and some have it on the twist-lock as well. A GFCI trips when it senses about 5 milliamps of current leaking to ground instead of returning on the neutral. Moisture is exactly the kind of thing that creates that leak: a film of water across the receptacle face, condensation inside a cord plug, a damp extension cord lying in wet grass, or a wet appliance on the far end. In humid weather during a storm, nuisance trips are common and they are the GFCI working as designed.
        </p>

        <p>
          The fix is to remove the moisture, not the protection. Shut the generator down. Unplug everything and dry the receptacle faces and the plug blades with a cloth. Get the cord connections off the ground and under the canopy, or wrap them in a cord connection cover — the rubber or plastic clamshell boxes sold for outdoor Christmas lights work well. Plug loads back in one at a time to find the one that trips it; a wet refrigerator compressor, a sump pump with a nicked cord, or an old fridge in the garage are frequent culprits. Never defeat the GFCI, plug an adapter in to bypass it, or move the load to a non-GFCI outlet to "fix" the trip. The leak that trips the GFCI is the shock hazard.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cords</h3>
        <p>
          Use outdoor-rated cords — SJTW or better on the jacket — of adequate gauge for the load and distance. Twelve gauge for 20 amps up to about 50 feet, ten gauge for longer runs or a 30 amp twist-lock. Undersized cord heats up, drops voltage and makes motor loads struggle. Route the cord so it does not lie in standing water, and keep every plug-to-socket junction elevated: over a bucket, on a paver, hung on a hook under the canopy. Water finds its way into a connection lying on the ground within minutes in heavy rain. If you are running a cord into the house, do it through a window or door that can be closed on it, not left open — and never through the door nearest the generator.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Your hands and feet</h3>
        <p>
          Dry your hands before touching the panel, and wear rubber-soled shoes rather than standing barefoot or in wet socks on wet ground. Plug and unplug cords with the generator running only at the generator end and only if the panel is dry; better still, shut it down to change connections. If you have any doubt about the ground path — the unit is sitting in mud, the frame is wet, the cord is wet — shut it down before you touch anything. The <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a> guide explains why a proper inlet and switch is safer than a tangle of extension cords in exactly these conditions.
        </p>

        <h2 id="snow" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Snow, Cold and Refuelling
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Snow and ice</h3>
        <p>
          Everything above applies in snow, with additions. Falling snow melts on a warm generator and runs into the same places rain does, so the canopy stays. Snow accumulates against the sides of the unit and can block the intake and bury the exhaust; check it every couple of hours in a heavy fall and keep a cleared zone of a few feet all round. Do not set the unit in a snowbank or a spot where roof snow will slide onto it. Exhaust that hits a snow wall bounces back toward the operator and, in a confined spot, toward the house — the 20 foot clearance from openings is more important in winter, not less, because windows are shut and CO that gets in stays in. Ice on the panel is the same hazard as water on the panel.
        </p>

        <p>
          Cold also affects starting. Use the oil weight the manual specifies for the temperature — 5W-30 synthetic for most units below freezing — and keep the battery on an electric-start unit charged, because a cold battery has far less cranking power. Fuel that has sat since the last outage may have absorbed moisture that freezes in the fuel line; fresh gasoline with stabiliser, or propane on a dual-fuel unit, avoids that. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">dual-fuel generator guide</a> covers the propane side.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Refuelling in the rain</h3>
        <p>
          Two rules, and the first one has nothing to do with rain. Shut the generator down and let it cool for 10 to 15 minutes before refuelling. Gasoline spilled on a hot muffler ignites, and people in a hurry during a storm have been badly burned that way. The second rule is to keep water out of the tank. Refuel under the canopy or hold an umbrella over the filler. Wipe the filler neck and cap dry before opening. Pour from a can with a proper spout rather than a funnel that catches rain. Water in gasoline sinks to the bottom of the tank, where the pickup is, and it causes stalling, surging and eventually a rusty carburettor bowl. A splash of fuel stabiliser with a water-dispersing component in the can helps with small amounts. Wipe any spilled fuel off the frame before restarting.
        </p>

        <p>
          Have enough fuel on hand before the storm. A 5,000 to 7,500 watt generator burns roughly half a gallon to three quarters of a gallon of gasoline an hour at half load, which is 12 to 18 gallons a day. Filling cans at a gas station in a storm, if the station even has power, is where a lot of avoidable risk gets taken.
        </p>

        <h2 id="soaked" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. CO Detectors and What to Do If It Got Soaked
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">CO detectors indoors</h3>
        <p>
          A generator running outdoors, 20 feet away, under a canopy, exhaust pointed away — done correctly, that setup should never put CO in your house. You still install CO alarms, because the whole point of a backup is that things do not go to plan. Battery-powered or battery-backed CO alarms on every level of the house and outside the sleeping areas, tested before the storm, with fresh batteries. Many newer portable generators also carry a built-in CO sensor that shuts the engine down if it detects CO accumulating around the unit; Generac calls it CO-Sense, Champion calls it CO Shield, Honda has CO-Minder. It is worth having, and it is also worth understanding that it protects against a generator in an enclosed space, not against exhaust drifting toward an open window 15 feet away. Your indoor alarms cover that.
        </p>

        <p>
          If a CO alarm sounds, get everyone outside first and shut the generator down second. Symptoms — headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion — are easy to mistake for tiredness in the middle of a long outage night, which is why the alarm matters.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If the generator got soaked</h3>
        <p>
          It happens: the canopy blew off, the unit was left out overnight uncovered, the yard flooded. The first rule is do not start it. A wet generator that starts may run — and it may also short the alternator or the inverter module and turn a drying-out job into a repair bill, or put current on the frame.
        </p>

        <p>
          Unplug every cord. Move the generator under cover, ideally somewhere with airflow. Open the outlet covers, remove the air filter and check whether the filter and the intake are wet, and pull the spark plug if you suspect water went down the intake. Pull the oil dipstick: oil that looks milky or foamy has water in it and must be changed before any start. Leave the unit for at least 24 hours, longer in humid weather, with a fan blowing across it if you can. Tip it slightly so water drains out of the panel and the alternator housing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then inspect. Look inside the receptacles for standing water, look at the control panel for condensation behind the breaker window, and check that the choke and throttle move freely. Start it with nothing plugged in and let it run for ten minutes to drive out the last moisture with engine heat. If it runs rough, trips its own breaker, or the GFCI will not reset, the alternator or the inverter has water in it, and a small-engine shop is the next stop. On an inverter unit, that repair may cost a large fraction of a new machine.
        </p>

        <p>
          The lesson, as always, is that the 150 dollar canopy is cheaper than any of this. Buy it with the generator, store it with the generator, and put it on before you pull the starter cord. That way the question in the title has a straightforward answer.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Moving it into the garage to keep it dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">An open door does not clear the CO. This is the setup behind most generator deaths in the US, and no fan or door arrangement makes it safe.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Draping a tarp over a running unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It traps heat and exhaust against the engine, blocks the intake, and melts or catches fire on the muffler. A cover must be a roof, not a wrap.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Bypassing the GFCI when it trips</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The trip means current is leaking to ground through water. Removing the protection removes the warning, not the hazard.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Cord connections lying in the wet grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Water gets into the plug junction within minutes in heavy rain. Elevate every connection and cover it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Refuelling hot to save time in the storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Gasoline on a hot muffler ignites. Shut down, wait ten to fifteen minutes, and keep the rain out of the tank while you pour.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run a portable generator in the rain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Not uncovered. Every manufacturer's manual says do not operate in rain or wet conditions because water on the outlets, control panel and alternator creates an electrocution and short-circuit risk. You can run it in the rain safely under a purpose-built generator canopy or an open-sided shelter that keeps water off the unit while leaving all four sides open for airflow and exhaust.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run my generator in the garage with the door open?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. An open door does not move enough air to clear the carbon monoxide, and CO drifts into the house through the connecting door, gaps and ductwork. Hundreds of people in the US have died this way. Generators run outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from any window, door or vent.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the safest way to cover a running generator?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A canopy designed for running generators, such as a GenTent, IGAN or a manufacturer's running cover, which mounts to the frame and sheds rain while leaving the sides fully open. A pop-up canopy with no side walls set well above the unit also works. Never use a tarp draped over the unit, a plastic storage shed, a box, or any enclosure with walls.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How far from the house should a generator be in a storm?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">At least 20 feet, per CDC and CPSC guidance, with the exhaust pointed away from the house and any neighbouring buildings. Storm wind can push exhaust back toward you, so more distance is better, and you should still have working CO alarms inside. Use a heavy-gauge outdoor extension cord rated for the load to cover the distance.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my generator's GFCI keep tripping in the rain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Moisture on the receptacle face, in the cord plug or in whatever you have plugged in is leaking a small current to ground, and the GFCI is doing its job. Dry the outlet and plug ends, get the cord connections up off the ground and under cover, and check the appliances on the circuit. Do not bypass the GFCI or swap to a non-GFCI outlet.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I refuel a generator while it is raining?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Shut it down and let it cool for at least 10 to 15 minutes first, rain or not, because fuel spilled on a hot muffler ignites. Refuel under the canopy so water does not run into the tank, wipe the filler neck dry, and pour from a can with a proper spout. Water in gasoline causes rough running and stalling and can corrode the carburettor.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I do if my generator got soaked?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Do not start it. Unplug everything, move it under cover, and let it dry completely for at least 24 hours, ideally with the outlet covers open and a fan on it. Then inspect the outlets, control panel and air filter for water, check the oil for a milky colour that means water got into the crankcase, and only then attempt a start with nothing connected.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run a standby generator in the rain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. Standby units from Generac, Kohler, Briggs and Stratton and Cummins live outdoors permanently inside a weatherproof enclosure that is designed for rain, snow and wind. They start automatically during storms and need nothing from you. The only maintenance-related caution is keeping the intake and exhaust louvres clear of snow and debris.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

