        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Yes, you can run a generator in the rain — under a canopy built for the job or an open-sided roof, on a dry pad, 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away and the cords elevated. The manual's warning is about water on the electrics, and a proper cover answers it.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            What you can never do is bring it indoors, into the garage, onto the porch or into a shed to keep it dry. A wet generator is a repair. A generator in an enclosed space is a funeral. Buy the cover with the machine and the choice never comes up.
          </p>
